  "text" : "But none of those pounds are guesses.\nEveryone comes out of a real Monte Carlo run.\nTen thousand iterations per estimate, not a single point number.\nAnd the model doesn't get to mark its own homework.\nWe backtest it against real incidents, the same credibility maths insurers use to blend a new claim against history.\nRight now on Lidlow's book, the model called £1 million, the real losses came in at £130,000.\nVerdict, overpriced.\nRecalibrate down, that's not a slide claiming honesty.\nThat's the engine catching itself being wrong, and it doesn't get a pass either.\nThe same engine prices, the platform's own risk, Kyverno, Flux, the platform code, all in scope, right now.\nThat book comes to over £1 million over its own £10,000 tolerance, so its own rule fires deny controls mandatory.\nWith those controls applied, residual risk\nDrops to £5,500 inside the band, it passes its own test on its own terms, or it doesn't get to run."
